{"content":"VERDICT: REVISE\n\nBLOCKERS:\n1. Frozen-consumer bypass risk. The candidate admits AOL Assurance has a strict consumer frozen to Orcastrata 1.0.0, then proposes a 'versioned consumer revision.' It never defines who owns the revision, who approves the relaxation of the frozen constant, or how the override is recorded as an authority effect distinct from a code edit. Without that, milestone 9 cannot pass acceptance and a future contributor can silently loosen the constant.\n2. Transport switching after effect start is underspecified. 'Do not silently switch after a possibly started effect' is a rule, not a mechanism. No pin-to-transport artifact is named, no per-effect transport record exists in the receipt, and no reconciliation check verifies that the recorded transport matches the one that actually issued the effect. A retry on a different transport will look idempotent by key but diverge by semantics.\n3. Idempotency key derivation is brittle across workspaces. Workspace identity is referenced but never anchored. Same umbrella revision reused across workspaces or clones yields colliding keys unless workspace provenance includes repo-root fingerprint plus HEAD umbrella SHA plus AOL umbrella-graph node UUID. Receipts bind keys, but replay against an evicted AOL umbrella-graph rebuild loses provenance.\n4. Unknown-outcome fencing leaks retries via webhook/events. Reconciliation fences retries until GitHub confirms applied/not-applied. The candidate does not state whether event-driven PR monitoring can independently fire a follow-up effect before reconciliation closes the fence. There is no explicit precedence rule and no mutex between reconciliation and event-driven worker re-arm.\n5. Audit-full independence is not actually independent. Audit-full is described as read-only and uses 'aggregate receipts first; fetch detail only for missing, stale, failed, or contradictory gates.' That selection is performed by the same process that produced the receipts. There is no separate oracle, separate fetch path, or signature-verified receipt log. A compromised or buggy producer trivially passes its own audit.\n6. Merge admission has nine listed gates but no failure policy per gate. 'Unknown outcome blocks retry until reconciliation' is stated for effects, but merge admission against stale check/review state is not addressed. A passing check that is later marked stale or dismissed can be admitted before reconciliation catches the change. No 'snapshot-then-verify' hash of required-checks and approval IDs at decision time.\n7. Worker scope enforcement is asserted, not designed. 'It cannot change another branch, broaden scope, rewrite shared history, or merge' is policy. No pre-push hook, no allowed-paths guard on the worker harness, no branch ref-locking primitive, and no dry-run diff that proves scope before push. A fresh implementer has nothing concrete to build against.\n8. Fresh-implementer readiness is weak for milestone 3. The argv array is concrete, but the candidate never specifies the credential-blind contract as testable assertions: 'must not read ~/.config/gh/hosts.yml, must not invoke gh auth token, must not open ~/.netrc, must not inherit GH_TOKEN from parent env, must not log argv containing --with-token.' Without these as named negative tests, a future implementer can pass milestone 3 by stubbing gh.\n\nHIGH-VALUE CHANGES:\n1. Make the AOL Assurance consumer bump a first-class authority effect: name the revision artifact (consumer-revision-vN), require operator approval distinct from code merge, and store it in the umbrella graph next to the frozen-constant rationale. Milestone 9 acceptance then checks the artifact, not the source.\n2. Pin transport per effect and record it in the receipt alongside the idempotency key. Reconciliation compares observed GitHub actor/client_id to the pinned transport; mismatch is REVISE, not retry.\n3. Define workspace provenance concretely: workspace_id = sha256(repo_root_path || umbrella_graph_node_uuid || HEAD_umbrella_sha || operator_id). Include in idempotency key and in receipts.\n4. Add an explicit precedence rule: event-driven PR monitoring is read-only and cannot trigger new worker effects until the originating effect's reconciliation reaches APPLIED or NOT_APPLIED. Encode as a per-effect state machine in the umbrella graph, not a convention.\n5. Ship audit-full as a separate command path with its own fetch code path, its own cache namespace, and signature verification over the receipt log produced by execution. The selector that decides 'fetch detail' runs only after signature check passes. If verification fails, audit-full returns BLOCKED with reason 'receipt-log-integrity'.\n6. Snapshot merge preconditions atomically: at decision time, capture {check_run_ids, status, conclusion, completed_at}, {review_ids, state, submitted_at}, {head_sha, base_sha}, {required_status_checks_contexts}, and hash them into the merge decision. Reconciliation re-evaluates by hash before admission.\n7. Replace 'worker cannot broaden scope' policy with mechanism: pre-push allowed-paths guard computed from the issue contract's file_scope set, branch ref locked to the leased branch name, and a pre-push diff-vs-allowed-paths assertion. All three are tests, not documentation.\n8. Convert credential-blindness into an executable contract: a single module that wraps every gh invocation, asserts no credential files are read, redacts argv and stdout, and emits a fixed list of negative tests. Milestone 3 has a single binary pass/fail: 'gh-canary' plus 'gh-blindness-tests'.\n9. Drop 'later indexing and recall of accepted artifacts' from Graph Engineering as in-scope for this candidate. It is speculative for an integration whose audit-full is not yet independent. Add it as an explicit non-goal.\n10. Collapse PR babysitting triggers into one sentence: operator-invoked only until the event-and-authority contracts ship. Remove the 'event-driven' phrasing from the body of the plan so milestone 6 does not accidentally implement it.\n\nREJECTED COMPLEXITY:\n1. Two-transport abstraction before a second transport is exercised. The candidate justifies one boundary because two transports exist; in practice only `gh` is exercised in milestone 3, and the OAuth/API connector is already inside AOL. Keep `gh` as the only new code path; do not introduce a transport interface in milestone 3.\n2. 'Projection revision' as a separate field until it is consumed. The umbrella graph can carry a monotonic revision counter; the term 'projection revision' adds nothing until reconciliation consumes it.\n3. Context-budget measurement instrumentation in the first tranche. Important, but not on the critical path for milestone 3. Defer until bounded workers exist.\n4. Symbolic inherited runtime credential references for Codex. Already supported in AOL; do not re-specify it in this candidate.\n5. 'Scheduled wakeups come only after...' as a planning constraint rather than a code one. Either implement the gate or remove the sentence.\n\nSMALLEST SAFE FIRST TRANCHE (milestone 3 only, as the candidate already proposes, with the following tight scope):\n- Single new module `gh_readiness` inside AOL that runs the four fixed argv commands, captures structured output, and returns a readiness record {gh_present, gh_authenticated, remote_url, repo, default_branch, viewer_permission, captured_at}.\n- No writes, no token extraction, no env mutation, no credential file reads. Enforced by code and by negative tests listed below.\n- Records are persisted into a new audit log keyed by readiness-run id; the log is append-only and signed (HMAC over the record using a key derived from the workspace provenance defined above).\n- Single new command path invoked by operator: `aol gh-readiness --workspace <id>`. No event-driven trigger, no hook, no scheduled wakeup.\n- Negative tests, all must pass: (a) `gh auth token` is never reachable through the module API; (b) env vars matching `*TOKEN*`, `*GH_*`, `GITHUB_*` are scrubbed from child argv; (c) filesystem reads of ~/.config/gh, ~/.netrc, ~/.config/gh/hosts.yml are blocked by path guard; (d) stdout capture is run through a redaction filter before logging; (e) module refuses to run if argv contains `--with-token` or `-t`.\n- One isolated real read-only canary against a designated scratch repo, gated by operator-set `AOL_GH_CANARY_REPO`. The canary is the only network call. It produces one signed readiness record and exits.\n- Consumer revision artifact for AOL Assurance is created in this tranche as data only (no code change to the consumer), with operator approval recorded in the umbrella graph. Milestone 9 will read it; nothing in milestone 3 depends on it functionally.\n- No change to OAuth, no new write operations, no merge code, no PR monitor, no audit-full.\n\nThis tranche is sufficient to prove credential blindness, transport pinning discipline, and signed audit-log foundation without committing to any GitHub write surface.\n\nroute_identity echo: {\"actual_model\":\"minimaxai/minimax-m3\",\"actual_provider\":\"Command Code\",\"declared_route\":\"worker_commandcode_model\",\"fallback_used\":false,\"retry_count\":0}","route_identity":{"actual_model":"minimaxai/minimax-m3","actual_provider":"Command Code","declared_route":"worker_commandcode_model","fallback_used":false,"retry_count":0}}
